A||N||10||87ECO5||Ford Mustang owners - please respond||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||01-08-2001||01:27 PM||||Interested in hearing from owners of (or people having experiences with) '90's Ford Mustangs with the 5.0 liter V8.<P>I've found a 1992 LX 5.0 5-speed convertible with 84,000 miles and would like opinions of (and warnings about) this model.  I've always heard the 5.0 V8 is a good motor.  Is this true?  Any known problems with it?  How many miles can this motor accumulate before it's shot?  How about the rest of the car?  Is the Mustang generally a good car?  I don't care too much about fit and finish but I <B>AM</B> concerned about it leaving my wife and kids stranded on the side of the road.  What's the Mustang's overall reliability rating?  Is it easy to work on?  Are parts easy or hard to find?<P>Any information would be greatly appreciated.  Thanks in advance ...||||reg||5
Z||000001||BBBAD GT||01-08-2001||02:20 PM||||Yes, they are good reliable cars.  I would first see if its been ragged out, like beaten to death and put away wet.  Look at the motor to see of they 'has been' some mods done, normally when people go to sell their 5.0's, they put the stock stuff back on and sell the racing stuff.  Look under the car, mainly torque boxes, to see if they are wasted or not (they can break if repeated high rpm launches with slicks are done).  <P>As you know, there are thousands of parts avail for this car, and its easy as pie to work on compared to the Fiero.  If you know the previous owner, then I'd say you made it half home already.  Email me if you have any questions specifically I can answer.<P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">     <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||2birds||01-08-2001||02:25 PM||||Parts are cheap, thousands were built.  It's pre-OBD-II, so its's almost as easy to diagnose problems as a Fiero.  5-speed is more reliable than the AOD (and more fun to drive, IMHO).<BR>Consumer Reports type mags never like performance cars, neither do insurance companies, so I wouldn't expect too high a rating from them.  <BR>Back seat is kind of tight, even with small kids.  That's why I've been driving T-birds instead.  My only 5.0 was a 92, and the only problem I had was Oxygen sensors at 60k.<P>my $.02||||reg||1
Z||000003||zeusrekning||01-08-2001||04:41 PM||||Dad has a 93gt conv. Nice car but it is still a ford. I put a cd player in for my mom and the factory speaker cables did not match the aftermarket to factory conector i bought. And cant put a cd in unless you are in drive and mabey neutral.Nice car... mabey<BR>Nice ford... yes  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||william_thorniii||01-08-2001||05:59 PM||||EC05,<P>Well, I can respond to this 'cause as you know the wife owns a '93 Mustang LX convertable.  The first thing you must remember is that the Ford 302 (4.9L) engine was used in most of their cars at one time.  The Mustang, Thunderbird, Crown Victoria, F150...  The engine is damn near as bullet-proof as a good 'ol Chivvvy three-fitty.  Also this makes both bone-yard and aftermarket parts readily available.  <P>The problems with the Mustang is that Rach will not be comfortable riding in the back for very long and I do not think Lisa will like putting Megan (car seat and all) in the tiny back seat repeatedly.  Although these points can be said for virtually any two door car and certainly more so for any two door convertible.  <P>As BB said, the hardest part is finding one that has not been beat... severely.  Another problem that I foresee is the fact that they are EXTREMELY difficult to control in the snow.  I know you would have 1 set of tires and the width required to hook up on dry pavement make it difficult to cut through the wet and snow.  The rear end is very light and makes for a dangerous combination of too much power and too little traction in many cases.  Of course Lisa likes driving hard and that helps the probability that she would know how to drive it when she needs to... either way I would think about it.  Linds has problems keeping her rear wheels from spinning on gravel, in rain, snow, ice, etc (don't you LOVE Ohio weather?).   <P>Linds' biggest complaint about her Mustang?... no cup holder.  Sure it is petty, but you have to admit, that especially with 2 kids and a clumsy husband (lol) that cupholders will help keep the interior clean.  <P>Overall ride and drive I think the Mustang does fairly well.  It does flex a bit, but not nearly as much as our old Cav convertable or the '00 Sebring convertable.  <P>LMK if you want to go for a ride in "Betty" (Lind's car). <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000005||WampusCat||01-09-2001||10:29 AM||||My wife had a '92 GT that she just sold in Sep with 136,000 mi.  Had the auto trans rebuilt at 120,000 and replaced some hoses and belts and shocks but no other problems.  Engine still ran like new and never used any oil.  The back seat on the 92 has more headroom than the new mustangs, but it's still kinda snug. The GT suspension on the mustangs back then were HARSH!  I would definitely go with an LX 5.0 if I had the choice.  Oh yeah, it got pretty good mileage with the 4spd auto. Better than my Fiero GT.||||reg||1
Z||000006||87ECO5||01-11-2001||02:25 PM||||When did they go from carbs to fuel injection?||||reg||5
Z||000007||2birds||01-11-2001||09:53 PM||||86 was SEFI (the good stuff) for all 5.0 Mustangs, the Holley 4-barrel was 83-85 manual transmissions, and the 85 Automatics got throttle body (not sure on earlier automatics.<BR>The SEFI years are split into two groups:  Speed Density, for 86-88, and Mass Air, for 89-up.  California 88's got mass air, too.<P>Other big diff's were the air bags beginning in 90 (no more tilt wheel until the 94 body style) and the 16 inch 5 spoke wheels in 91-93.  T-tops went away after 88.  The 8.8 inch rear end was standard from 86 or 87 on and the T-5's got improved slightly over the years.||||reg||1
Z||000008||BBBAD GT||01-12-2001||09:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WampusCat:<BR><B> The GT suspension on the mustangs back then were HARSH!  I would definitely go with an LX 5.0 if I had the choice.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Both suspensions were the same.  The only difference between the GT and the 5.0 LX were the body stuff, the motor/suspension were 'exactly' the same.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">     <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Haze_Performance||01-13-2001||04:52 AM||||The 92'<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/008_8.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/009_9_1.jpg"> <P>The 88'<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88frontpass.jpg"> <P>The 86' Before the body work<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/86passside.jpg"> <P><BR>Ive had 3 5.0L's. I just got rid of my 92' GT Convert. today.(Long Story) I love the Fox Body Mustangs & if you would like to buy a "Like New" Chrome Kenny Brown strut tower brace (VERY CHEAP)let me know. I just bought it brand new for $270.00.<P>Go here to visit my Mustang site. <A HREF="http://haze_mustang_performance.com.drive.to/" TARGET=_blank>http://haze_mustang_performance.com.drive.to/</A> <P>Im looking for a nice 93' 5-speed notchback (daily driver) now.<P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/goodlogo1_1.gif">        <BR><B>"AHHHHH!!! All Fiero's don't start on fire you BONEHEAD!!"</B>||||reg||6
Z||000010||BBBAD GT||01-14-2001||08:57 AM||||I just bought an 86 SVO for my daily driver (it replaced the 87 Fiero GT, it has a backseat unlike the Fiero).  It only has 36k miles, and is a pretty neat toy.  <P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">     <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
